  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "Let the equation of the plane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> containing the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and the distance of the plane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> from the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>)</mo></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "355",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given,</p><p>The line &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;which passes through the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>Also given the&nbsp;equation of plane &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and the point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;will lie on it as plane contains the line,</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Now product of direction ratio of line and direction ratio of plane will be zero as they both are perpendicular,</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Now on solving equation&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;we get,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>Hence, the equation of plane will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>Now distance from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>from plane&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>54</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><msqrt><mn>14</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>70</mn><msqrt><mn>14</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><msqrt><mn>14</mn></msqrt></math></p><p>Hence,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>350</mn></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>355</mn></math></p>",
